          "text": "Of course. Here is an explanation of why CUDA graph replay can be faster than eager kernel launches and what the trade-offs are.\n\n### Why CUDA Graph Replay is Faster\n\nThe primary reason a CUDA graph replay is faster than launching the same kernels eagerly is the **reduction of CPU-side overhead**. When you launch kernels eagerly, the CPU must perform a series of tasks for each kernel: validate parameters, allocate resources, and issue a command to the GPU driver. This process involves significant CPU work and context switching, which introduces latency. For a workload with many small, fast-running kernels, this CPU overhead can become the bottleneck, leaving the GPU waiting for its next instruction.\n\nA CUDA graph, by contrast, pre-compiles a sequence of kernel launches and their dependencies into a single, immutable structure. When you \"replay\" this graph, the CPU issues just **one command** to the GPU driver to execute the entire sequence. The GPU's scheduler then takes over, launching the kernels in the pre-defined order with minimal CPU intervention. This effectively amortizes the CPU launch overhead across all the kernels in the graph, allowing the GPU to be fed work much more efficiently and keeping its execution units saturated.\n\n### The Costs and Trade-offs\n\nThe main cost of using CUDA graphs is",
